n\":\"cache\"}); Total lines: 441\nL198: ## Documentation\nL199: \nL200: Deeper technical write-ups — proof protocols for each layer (embeddings, QKV, multi-head attention, positional encoding, LayerNorm, RMSNorm, argmax), softmax, EinSum, ReLU, MaxPool, range checks, lookup tables, and commitment schemes — live in 【57†`docs/`】 and are built with 【58†mdBook】 + 【59†mdbook-katex】:\nL201: \nL202:     cargo install mdbook\nL203:     cargo install mdbook-katex\nL204:     mdbook build docs --open\nL205: \nL206: ## Supported Models & Layers\nL207: ### Transformer (LLM)\nL208: \nL209: The recommended path is SafeTensors via Hugging Face: pass `--hf <model-id>` and `bench-llm` will fetch and cache the weights automatically on first run. GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX models are also supported.\nL210: Model  | Recommended loader  | Notes\nL211: --- | --- | ---\nL212: GPT-2  | `--hf openai-community/gpt2`  | SafeTensors auto-downloaded from HF. GGUF also supported (`--gguf gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`)\nL213: Gemma 3  | `--hf google/gemma-3-1b-it`  | SafeTensors only\nL214: Llama 2  | `--hf meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf`  | SafeTensors only. `--accuracy` mode currently falls back to default quantization for Llama 2\nL215: \nL216: Supported transformer layers:\nL217:   * Embeddings — provable token vocabulary lookup\nL218:   * Positional encoding — Absolute and Rotary (RoPE)\nL219:   * Attention mask — causal masking for auto-regressive generation\nL220:   * EinSum / Multi-head attention — batched matrix multiplication for QK^T and OV projections\nL221:   * Softmax — with provable range checks\nL222:   * LayerNorm / RMSNorm — vector normalization with proofs\nL223:   * Argmax / Logits — next-token prediction output layer\nL224: ### Traditional (MLP / CNN)\nL225: \nL226:   * Dense — fully connected layers\nL227:   * Conv2D — convolutions with FFT-based optimization\nL228:   * Activations — ReLU, GELU\nL229:   * Pooling — MaxPool\nL230:   * Reshape / Flatten / Add — tensor manipulation and residual connections\nL231: \nL232: ## Installation\nL233: \nL234: ### Prerequisites\nL235: \nL236:   * Rust + Cargo (latest stable)\nL237:   * Python 3.10+ — only required if you plan to run the MLP/CNN benchmark harness `bench.py`\nL238:   * CUDA 12.x toolkit + NVIDIA driver — only required for the GPU build\nL247: Feature  | Platform  | Effect\nL248: --- | --- | ---\nL249: `cuda`  | Linux + NVIDIA  | Replaces the CPU PCS with `HyperKZGGpu` and runs inference on burn's CUDA backend — fastest path\nL250: `wgpu`  | Linux / macOS / Windows  | Cross-platform GPU inference; PCS still runs on CPU\nL251: \nL252:     # Linux + NVIDIA: full GPU acceleration (inference + PCS)\nL253:     cargo build --release --features cuda -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL254: \nL255:     # Cross-platform GPU inference only\nL256:     cargo build --release --features wgpu -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL257: The default (no `--features` flag) is a CPU-only build using the `ndarray` backend.\nL258: ## Quickstart: Prove GPT-2 Inference End-to-End\nL259: \nL260: This walkthrough builds the binary, fetches GPT-2 from Hugging Face, and produces an end-to-end inference proof.\nL261: \nL262: ### 1. Build\nL263: \nL264:     cargo build --release -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL265: ### 2. Get a GPT-2 model\nL266: \nL267: The recommended path is `--hf openai-community/gpt2`: `bench-llm` will fetch the SafeTensors weights and tokenizer from Hugging Face on first run and cache them locally for subsequent runs — no manual download required. SafeTensors is the most widely-used checkpoint format for transformer models, which is why we lead with it.\nL268: GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX checkpoints are also supported; the GGUF path is convenient if you already have a pre-quantized file (e.g. `gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`).\nL269: ### 3. Prove a single sequence\nL270: \nL271:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL272:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64\nL273: \nL274: This builds the proving context, runs inference on a 64-token random prompt, generates a proof, and verifies it. Two CSV files are produced (see Reading the output below).\nL275: ### 4. Sweep multiple sequence lengths\nL276: \nL277:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL278:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64,128,256,512\nL279: \nL280: Each sequence length appends one row to the bench CSV.\nL281: \nL282: ### 5. Run on GPU\nL283: \nL284: Linux + NVIDIA:\nL285: \nL286:     cargo run --release --features cuda --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL287:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64\nL288: \nL289: The `cuda` feature switches the PCS to `HyperKZGGpu` and runs inference on the GPU backend.\nL290: ### 6. Cache the proving context\nL291: \nL292: The proving context (`pk` / `vk`) is the most expensive one-off step. Save it to disk once and reuse it across runs:\nL293: \nL294:     # First run: generate and save\nL295:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL296:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 \\\nL297:       --save_params setup\nL298: \nL299:     # Subsequent runs: skip context generation\nL300:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL301:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 \\\nL302:       --load_params setup\nL303: This writes/reads `setup.pk` and `setup.vk` in the current directory.\nL304: ### 7. Higher-accuracy mode\nL305: \nL306:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL307:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 --accuracy\nL308: \nL309: `--accuracy` enables outlier smoothing and per-tensor activation tracking, improving cosine similarity / perplexity at the cost of additional proving time. Available for GPT-2 and Gemma 3.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2607.08095] zkComposer: Decomposing Proof Construction to Scale zkML (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08095)\nciteturn3view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 